WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:04.920
A background as a children's performer&nbsp;
and drawing inspiration from Mr. Bean? The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:09.240
person behind Art the Clown's heavy makeup&nbsp;
looks completely different out of costume.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:14.160
Pennywise isn't the only circus performer killing&nbsp;
it in the horror genre these days. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:18.780
Pennywise's reputation as the scariest&nbsp;
clown in pop culture is arguably being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:24.360
contested by another haunting persona that is&nbsp;
reportedly causing audiences to faint and vomit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:29.640
in movie theaters, per Evening Standard. The&nbsp;
critically acclaimed "Terrifier 2," directed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:34.140
by Damien Leone, sees the return of&nbsp;
the seriously creepy Art the Clown.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:39.300
The character first appeared in Leone's&nbsp;
2013 anthology flick "All Hallows Eve," in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:43.680
which Art was played by Mike Giannelli. The&nbsp;
first full-length "Terrifier" film starring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:49.560
David Howard Thornton as Art was unleashed&nbsp;
upon audiences in 2016. The end result was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:53.940
an 86-minute gore-filled bloodbath of&nbsp;
terror featuring a nightmare-inducing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:58.200
menace in the form of Art the Clown. The&nbsp;
low-budget feature may not have all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:02.580
bells and whistles of most big-name horror&nbsp;
flicks, but Leone somehow managed to max&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:06.960
out the mayhem in every frame, thanks&nbsp;
in part to well-crafted monster, Art.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:12.540
The creepy clown killer has received significant&nbsp;
acclaim since his debut, with Farsighted claiming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:17.100
that Art makes Stephen King's Pennywise look&nbsp;
more like Krusty the Clown from "The Simpsons."

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:18.863
"Hey kids!" 

00:01:21.259 --> 00:01:22.080
It goes without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:26.460
saying that the actor behind the iconic makeup&nbsp;
deserves a lot of credit for helping director&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:31.920
Leone realize his goal of outdoing the first film&nbsp;
with the sequel. And after fans watched the ending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:36.900
of "Terrifier 2," they may have been curious to&nbsp;
know what Art the Clown looks like out of costume.

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:41.940
Actor David Howard Thornton plays Art the&nbsp;
Clown in both "Terrifier" and "Terrifier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:47.580
2." Thornton's career is made up of an assortment&nbsp;
of credits including theater and voice-over work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:52.680
which don't just involve homicidal clowning&nbsp;
around. He has appeared in the DC drama series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:57.660
"Gotham," and has also done voice-over work&nbsp;
for the Hulu animated series "The Bravest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:01.980
Knight." But after perusing his resume,&nbsp;
it quickly becomes clear that Thornton's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:06.420
most notorious claim to fame to date is,&nbsp;
without a doubt, playing Art the Clown.

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:09.720
Much of the praise heaped upon&nbsp;
the "Terrifier" films recognize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:13.140
both the hideous onscreen actions&nbsp;
of the vile face-painted killer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:18.060
and the actor playing him. Starburst Magazine&nbsp;
commended the frightful clown, writing,

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:22.740
"Art is a truly enigmatic and memorable&nbsp;
villain. He frequently veers into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:26.820
territory of being genuinely unpleasant&nbsp;
to watch, which makes him feel somewhat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:30.780
separate from the stable of horror icons&nbsp;
such as Freddy Krueger and Chucky."

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:34.740
But the character can't take all of&nbsp;
the credit, and the publication also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:37.320
praised Thornton's brilliant performance, writing,

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:42.180
"Special acknowledgment should be given to David&nbsp;
Howard Thornton for a truly wonderful performance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:46.740
and one that easily stands toe-to-toe&nbsp;
with the likes of Curry and Skarsgård."

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:50.520
It might seem like playing a terrifying&nbsp;
clown murderer is the farthest thing away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:53.220
from kid-friendly entertainment. But for Thornton,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:56.220
his past work as a performer&nbsp;
for children came in handy.

00:02:56.220 --> 00:03:01.020
Thornton revealed to Dread Central that he&nbsp;
scored the horrifying role by auditioning on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:06.180
Actor's Access, with the call-out asking for tall&nbsp;
and skinny performers with clowning or physical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:11.340
comedy experience. While Thornton was new to&nbsp;
clowning, physical comedy was in his wheelhouse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:16.380
and his years doing children's theater made him&nbsp;
stand out to the casting director. According to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:20.880
Backstage, Thornton previously studied at&nbsp;
the Fantasy Playhouse Children's Theater in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:24.900
Huntsville, Alabama, which unexpectedly&nbsp;
helped prepare him for the "Terrifier."

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:28.980
"I would honestly like to make one more&nbsp; 
and have an idea for a solid trilogy."

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:34.200
Given that Art the Clown doesn't have any lines,&nbsp;
Thornton's physical comedy training came in very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:39.180
handy. In addition to his children's theater&nbsp;
experience, he also drew inspiration from other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:45.300
great physical comedians, such as Charlie Chaplin&nbsp;
and Rowan Atkinson, who plays Mr. Bean. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:49.320
Thornton also based his performance on some&nbsp;
of the greatest horror actors of all time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:54.240
like Robert Englund, who played Freddy Krueger,&nbsp;
and Kane Hodder who portrayed Jason Voorhees.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:59.700
All of these many influences perfectly came&nbsp;
together to create a truly killer clown.

